<h1><span class="part">Part F</span>What we can and cannot claim</h1>

## F1. What we can claim

On the team's own 9 cases, with nothing tuned and the plan fixed before running:

1. **A new, prediction-free rule makes both LR-MMT versions use less energy in every
   case.** Placing each task next to tasks of its own job changed energy by {{ v.S1.total }} for LR-MMT
   (it passes every pre-set test) and by {{ v.S2.total }} for the memory-aware version ({{ v.S2.large }} on the
   largest workload).
2. **It removes most of the memory-aware version's large-workload penalty.** Against LR-MMT,
   that version went from {{ v.size.lr_mmt_memory.s4000 }} to {{ v.size.lr_mmt_memory_same_job.s4000 }} on the largest workload.
3. **Its assumption is strongly supported:** job-mates finish a median of {{ v.premise.same_gap }} apart,
   against {{ v.premise.other_gap }} for other tasks released at the same time.
4. **It works the way we said it would:** busy servers held fewer different jobs, and
   {{ v.pred_held }} of our {{ v.pred_total }} predictions came true (E8).

How big is this? For plain LR-MMT, {{ v.S1.total }} is modest. For the memory-aware version the gain
is large, but **First Fit still uses the least energy on the largest workload**, and the
memory-aware comparison misses the team's crowding limit in one case by {{ v.s2_over_by }} points. The
honest headline is: *the rule improves both LR-MMT versions in all 9 cases; First Fit still
wins the largest workload.*

